1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,975
An AI assistant is only as useful as the tools and information it can reach.

2
00:00:05,975 --> 00:00:11,374
This video shows how the Model Context Protocol connects your assistants to outside

3
00:00:11,374 --> 00:00:15,803
tools, and how your site can share its own tools, prompts and files

4
00:00:15,803 --> 00:00:17,320
with other AI apps.

5
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:25,001
An assistant that can only talk is limited.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,215
To be useful, it has to look things up, run a calculation, or

7
00:00:29,215 --> 00:00:30,850
read a company file.

8
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,727
Those tools and files live in many places:

9
00:00:34,727 --> 00:00:40,720
a time-zone service, a ticket system, a shared drive, or an app on someone's computer.

10
00:00:41,470 --> 00:00:45,178
Building a custom link to each one would take a long time, and

11
00:00:45,178 --> 00:00:47,250
every AI app would need its own.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,025
The Model Context Protocol, or MCP, is a common standard.

13
00:00:52,025 --> 00:00:56,242
Any tool server that speaks it can be plugged in, and any AI

14
00:00:56,242 --> 00:00:58,990
app that speaks it can use your site.

15
00:01:03,190 --> 00:01:05,348
Your site can be an MCP client.

16
00:01:05,348 --> 00:01:09,723
A connection brings the tools of an outside server into the site, and

17
00:01:09,723 --> 00:01:12,290
any assistant you choose can use them.

18
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,700
Your site can also be an MCP server.

19
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:20,260
You choose which of your site's tools to share with other AI apps.

20
00:01:21,010 --> 00:01:25,911
A server can also offer prompts, which are ready-made instructions with blanks to

21
00:01:25,911 --> 00:01:29,910
fill in, and resources, which are files and data an app can read.

22
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:33,973
Each part is its own feature, so you only see the menus of

23
00:01:33,973 --> 00:01:35,940
the features that are turned on.

24
00:01:40,118 --> 00:01:44,907
Open Artificial Intelligence, Model Context Protocol, MCP Hosts.

25
00:01:44,907 --> 00:01:48,328
The connections of the site are listed here.

26
00:01:48,781 --> 00:01:50,341
Click Add Connection.

27
00:01:50,341 --> 00:01:53,633
The window lists the kinds of server you can reach.

28
00:01:53,633 --> 00:01:57,561
Click Add on Server-Sent Events, which is a server on the web.

29
00:01:58,012 --> 00:02:03,582
Give the connection a Title, and enter the Endpoint, which is the address of the server.

30
00:02:04,032 --> 00:02:07,785
Under Authentication, choose how the site signs in.

31
00:02:07,785 --> 00:02:10,221
Anonymous suits an open server.

32
00:02:10,221 --> 00:02:12,722
This server asks for an API key.

33
00:02:13,175 --> 00:02:17,973
You can set the Header Name and a Key Prefix, then paste the API key.

34
00:02:17,973 --> 00:02:21,235
It is stored securely, and never shown again.

35
00:02:21,687 --> 00:02:22,645
Click Save.

36
00:02:22,645 --> 00:02:26,532
The site does not contact the server yet, so a wrong address or

37
00:02:26,532 --> 00:02:30,307
key shows up the first time an assistant uses the connection.

38
00:02:34,492 --> 00:02:38,100
The other kind of connection is Standard Input/Output.

39
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:43,152
Instead of an address, it starts a program that runs on the site's own server.

40
00:02:43,603 --> 00:02:47,248
Enter a Title and the Command that starts the program.

41
00:02:47,248 --> 00:02:53,443
The optional fields are the Command arguments, the Working directory, and Environment variables.

42
00:02:53,896 --> 00:02:59,005
This kind is offered only when the Model Context Protocol Local Client feature is on.

43
00:02:59,005 --> 00:03:02,542
It runs a program on your server, so turn it on only when

44
00:03:02,542 --> 00:03:04,676
your technical team asks for it.

45
00:03:05,128 --> 00:03:06,148
Click Save.

46
00:03:06,148 --> 00:03:11,248
Both kinds of connection now appear in the list, each with its connection type.

47
00:03:15,448 --> 00:03:17,348
Anonymous needs no sign-in.

48
00:03:17,348 --> 00:03:21,263
Use it only for servers that are open to everyone, or that sit

49
00:03:21,263 --> 00:03:22,818
on a private network.

50
00:03:23,568 --> 00:03:28,510
API Key sends a key in a header, and Basic Authentication sends a

51
00:03:28,510 --> 00:03:30,668
user name and a password.

52
00:03:31,418 --> 00:03:37,199
The OAuth 2.0 options get a token from the provider's Token Endpoint, using

53
00:03:37,199 --> 00:03:42,338
a Client ID with a secret, a private key, or a client certificate.

54
00:03:43,088 --> 00:03:46,606
Custom Headers adds any headers the server asks for.

55
00:03:46,606 --> 00:03:51,641
Secrets are always stored securely, and a blank secret field keeps the stored

56
00:03:51,641 --> 00:03:53,218
value when you edit.

57
00:03:57,398 --> 00:04:01,054
A connection does nothing until an assistant uses it.

58
00:04:01,054 --> 00:04:06,258
Open Artificial Intelligence, Profiles, and edit the profile of the assistant.

59
00:04:06,716 --> 00:04:12,096
On the Capabilities tab, find MCP Connections and tick the connection.

60
00:04:12,546 --> 00:04:13,575
Click Save.

61
00:04:13,575 --> 00:04:18,174
The assistant now sees the tools of that server, and decides by itself

62
00:04:18,174 --> 00:04:19,626
when to call them.

63
00:04:20,076 --> 00:04:24,579
Profile templates and chat interactions have the same list, so you can offer

64
00:04:24,579 --> 00:04:26,556
a connection wherever it fits.

65
00:04:30,731 --> 00:04:37,431
To share your site's tools, open Settings, Artificial Intelligence, and find the MCP Server section.

66
00:04:37,885 --> 00:04:42,266
The Authentication type decides how other apps sign in:

67
00:04:42,266 --> 00:04:47,007
OpenID Connect, API key, or None, which is anonymous access.

68
00:04:47,007 --> 00:04:49,305
Use None only for testing.

69
00:04:49,756 --> 00:04:54,075
With an API key, enter the key that other apps must send.

70
00:04:54,075 --> 00:05:01,206
With OpenID Connect, Require access permission also asks for the Access the MCP Server permission.

71
00:05:01,659 --> 00:05:04,148
Nothing is shared until you pick it.

72
00:05:04,148 --> 00:05:08,119
Tick the tools to share under Tools, or use Select All Tools.

73
00:05:08,569 --> 00:05:12,991
Expose all tools shares every tool and every tool instance.

74
00:05:12,991 --> 00:05:16,869
Use it only when you trust every app that connects.

75
00:05:17,323 --> 00:05:18,368
Click Save.

76
00:05:18,368 --> 00:05:23,903
The site reloads its settings, and the chosen tools are available to connected apps.

77
00:05:28,123 --> 00:05:33,376
Prompts are ready-made instructions that other AI apps can offer to their users.

78
00:05:33,376 --> 00:05:38,873
Open Artificial Intelligence, Model Context Protocol, Prompts, and click Add Prompt.

79
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:44,746
Enter a Title, a unique Name, and a Description that says what the prompt does.

80
00:05:44,746 --> 00:05:48,759
Other apps use the Name to ask for the prompt, so keep it

81
00:05:48,759 --> 00:05:50,733
short and without spaces.

82
00:05:51,190 --> 00:05:53,001
Arguments are the blanks.

83
00:05:53,001 --> 00:05:57,149
Click Add Argument, give it a Name, and mark it Required when the

84
00:05:57,149 --> 00:05:58,610
app must supply it.

85
00:05:59,077 --> 00:06:02,200
Under Messages, add the text that is sent.

86
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:06,233
Choose a Role, User or Assistant, and write the Content.

87
00:06:06,233 --> 00:06:12,087
An argument's name in double curly braces is replaced by the value the app supplies.

88
00:06:12,554 --> 00:06:13,636
Click Save.

89
00:06:13,636 --> 00:06:18,024
Any app connected to your MCP server can now offer this prompt.

90
00:06:22,224 --> 00:06:23,294
Live facts:

91
00:06:23,294 --> 00:06:30,214
connect a time-zone, weather or ticket service, so a support assistant answers from current information.

92
00:06:30,964 --> 00:06:32,104
Company tools:

93
00:06:32,104 --> 00:06:37,514
give your assistants the tools your technical team already built, through one connection.

94
00:06:38,264 --> 00:06:39,457
Other AI apps:

95
00:06:39,457 --> 00:06:43,630
let staff use their favorite AI app with the tools of your site,

96
00:06:43,630 --> 00:06:46,014
without a second copy of anything.

97
00:06:46,764 --> 00:06:48,071
Shared wording:

98
00:06:48,071 --> 00:06:53,174
publish the prompts every team should use, so everyone asks in the same way.

99
00:06:55,174 --> 00:06:59,286
Connect the tools you need, give them to the assistants that should use

100
00:06:59,286 --> 00:07:02,916
them, and share your own tools and prompts with other AI apps.

101
00:07:02,916 --> 00:07:05,906
Share only what you are comfortable having called.

102
00:07:05,906 --> 00:07:07,294
Thanks for watching.

